gh pr create --base main --milestone "v1.2 Week 7 — feature burn-down" --title "feat(orchestration): alert when a bound pipeline runs far longer or shorter than usual, or is overdue" --body-file - <<'EOF'
## Summary
Pipeline duration and cadence become a baselined signal, raised before any DQ check on the pipeline's data fails.

**Where it lives (the issue asked for a design note):** it monitors orchestration rows, not datasets, so it is **not a `check.kind`** (ADR 0012; orchestration providers are not datasources). It is:
- a new service, `services/pipeline_baseline.py`;
- a beat task, `check_pipeline_baselines`, every 10 minutes on the poll's cadence;
- a signal on the existing workspace-health delivered-alert path, one `workspace_health` row per pipeline, keyed by a hash because a pipeline id can be 256 characters.

`pipeline_runs` is already the history, so the answer to the issue's "parallel table or synthetic anchor?" is neither: no new table and **no migration**.

- **Duration:** the latest successful run is scored against the previous 20 successful runs of the same provider, pipeline and env, flagging 3 or more standard deviations either way. It needs at least 5 earlier runs. The spread is floored at 10% of the mean, so a clockwork pipeline is not flagged for a second's wobble. The floor also matters the other way: without it, zero variance scored every change as 0, so a 60 s → 600 s jump never alerted. A test covers exactly that, and removing the floor turns it red.
- **Overdue:** no successful run within the pipeline's cadence threshold (largest gap between successes plus 25%). This is the same definition #1648's freshness-threshold hint uses, so the two can't disagree.
- **Delivery:** the delivered-first rule from poll staleness applies. The failure edge is recorded only after a channel actually sends it (an undeliverable alert is retried every tick), and a recovery edge is sent once the pipeline is back within its baseline. Only pipelines with an enabled trigger binding are watched. `PIPELINE_BASELINE_Z_THRESHOLD` defaults to 3.0; 0 turns the check off.
- **Evidence card:** the ad-hoc last-10 average is replaced by the same score. `delay_seconds_vs_history` stays, now scoped to the run's env and computed over the last 20 runs, and `duration_z_score` and `duration_baseline_points` are added. One prior run is reported as a mean, never as a z-score.
- **Refactor:** `HealthPublisher.publish_poll_staleness` becomes `publish_workspace_signal(report: PollStalenessReport | PipelineBaselineReport)`. The Teams, Slack and email renderers dispatch on the report type through `render.signal_*`. The existing staleness tests pass unchanged apart from the rename, which shows the rename changed no behaviour.
- **Docs:** a new section in the notifications guide, with a worked example; a responsibility line in the orchestration guide; and `.env.app.example`.

## Tests
- `test_pipeline_baseline.py` (11 tests):
  - insufficient history;
  - slow versus normal;
  - clockwork wobble not flagged, clockwork jump flagged;
  - other envs and later runs excluded;
  - overdue against the cadence threshold;
  - alert once, then recover;
  - overdue alert;
  - undeliverable not recorded;
  - unbound pipeline ignored;
  - threshold 0 disables.
- **Mutation-checked:** removing the stddev floor turns the jump test red, and removing the env filter turns the env test red.
- `test_pipeline_baseline_render.py`: all three channels render the slow, fast, overdue and recovered headlines and facts.
- **Evidence card:** a new assertion covers the baseline fields.
- **Gates:** full backend suite 8059 passed; mypy on `backend/app` and `backend/tests` clean; strict docs build and reference-docs check clean.
- **Not verified:** delivery through a real channel. The publish path is the existing staleness path; this change only adds the renderer and report.

Fixes #1653

